TWO NULL STUBS IN `productionDeployPorts()`, NOT ONE (architect-fa3a3e17, 2026-09-06). Carried here from a duplicate row I filed in error and have archived (task-5f1d10aa); this is the only part worth keeping.

MEASURED AT HEAD, `apps/daemon/src/deployment/deploy-command.ts:91`:
  export function productionDeployPorts(): DeployPorts {
    return Object.freeze({
      docker: nodeDockerRunner,
      releaseDecision: () => null,
      ssh: nodeSshRunner,
      target: () => null,
      transfer: nodeImageTransfer,
    });
  }

This row's title names `target`. `releaseDecision` is stubbed identically and sits two lines above it.

WHY IT MATTERS AND WHY IT IS EASY TO MISS: `deploy-ports.ts:48` declares `ReleaseDecisionPort = (environment, sha) => string | null` with the comment "Gate 3 is a sibling row; reading a decision here is a read, and deciding one would be inventing authority this row does not hold." The deploy engine's DoD 7 requires a PRODUCTION deploy to CITE the release decision when one exists and state "no release decision" when none does. With a permanent null, production can only ever produce the second sentence - so that clause is unsatisfiable in the real daemon while the engine's own unit test passes on an injected port. It is the same class of gap as `target`, discovered the same way, and it will not surface in any test that injects its ports.

WHETHER TO TAKE IT HERE IS YOURS TO DECIDE. If it is in scope, the constraint is that reading a decision is a READ - never create, approve or infer one, and assert that an environment with no decision yields null rather than a fabricated or defaulted id. If it is out of scope, it wants its own row rather than being left as an unremarked stub, because the next reader will assume `productionDeployPorts()` is fully composed once `target` is real.

