```
And deploy-service.ts:230-232 refuses before any docker spawn:
```
const target = ports.target(environment);
if (target === null) {
  return refuse(null, DEPLOY_TARGET_MISSING, `no deploy target bound for ${environment}`);
}
```
The next line (:234) is the first docker call, so the refusal really is upstream of every spawn — do not reorder it (task rail 5).

EXACTLY ONE CALL SITE. `git grep -n "productionDeployPorts" -- apps packages` -> deploy-command.ts:91 (definition) and :190 (`ports: options.ports ?? productionDeployPorts(), projectId, store,`). Both `projectId` and `store` are already in scope there. No barrel export, no test caller.

THE PRODUCER, LANDED (task-358b6ec8, DONE, merged 6cf7f15c). deploy-target-command.ts:9-22 `setDeployTarget` commits `{network, sshTarget, url}` with `aggregateId: deployTargetAggregateId(request.projectId, admitted.environment)`, `eventType: DEPLOY_TARGET_BOUND_EVENT`, AND the same object as the decision `result`. Its QA reviewSummary confirms the durable properties you can rely on rather than re-prove: "replace-not-duplicate", "one current binding", "environments isolated", "CAS refusal preserving the target", "replay without a second event".

THE CONTRACTS (deploy-target-contracts.ts): `deployTargetAggregateId(projectId, environment)` :83 -> `deploy-target:<projectId>:<environment>`; `DEPLOY_TARGET_BOUND_EVENT = "EnvironmentDeployTargetBound"` :41; `decodeDeployTarget(value: JsonValue | undefined): DeployTarget | null` :117, which re-admits network/sshTarget/url under TODAY'S rules and returns a FROZEN object.

DEAD CODE WORTH NOT TOUCHING: `DEPLOY_ENVIRONMENT_UNBOUND` (:46) has ZERO consumers — `git grep -n DEPLOY_ENVIRONMENT_UNBOUND -- apps` returns only its own declaration. It is NOT the code for this path; the engine's refusal is `DEPLOY_TARGET_MISSING` from deploy-receipt-contracts.ts. Do not adopt it and do not delete it (out of scope).

THE PORT SHAPE (deploy-ports.ts): `DeployTarget` interface :33, `DeployTargetPort = (environment: string) => DeployTarget | null` :41, `readonly target: DeployTargetPort` :55. No change needed to this file.

THE TEST HARNESS THAT SATISFIES DoD 2 ALREADY EXISTS. deploy-journey.test.ts (424 lines) drives the REAL command edge: it resolves `entries[DEPLOYMENT_DEPLOY_COMMAND_KIND].asyncHandler` (:117) and `ports.registry.get(...)` (:362), and injects a `ports:` object at :110. That injection is exactly the seam for "real target reader + injected docker". Test files are exempt from the 250/400 cap, so growing it is fine.

STORE READ SHAPE: `store.readEvents(<aggregateId>)` is the read used against this aggregate throughout deploy-target-command.test.ts (:59, :80, :101, :111, :143). deploy-ledger.ts:193 shows `store.getAggregateVersion(aggregateId)` for the write side.

SIZES: deploy-command.ts 208, deploy-service.ts untouched, deploy-journey.test.ts 424 (test, exempt).
